---
/**
 * Visualizer — the interactive DSA visualization island (site spec §11.3, design §2).
 *
 * Props:
 * - `algorithm`        registry AlgorithmId (validated at BUILD time — unknown → build fails).
 * - `renderer`         registry RendererId (validated at BUILD time).
 * - `input`            optional initial input, e.g. "[1,3,5,7,9,11] target=7";
 *                      else the algorithm's defaultInput().
 * - `allowCustomInput` show the custom-input form (default true).
 * - `showMetrics`      show the metrics pills (default true).
 * - `inputLabel`/`inputPlaceholder`  label + placeholder for the FIRST field
 *                      (default "Array"). Non-array lessons override the label
 *                      (e.g. graph "Edges", recursion "n"); the placeholder is
 *                      the AUTHORED `input` whenever there is one, so the prop
 *                      is consulted only by a viz without an `input` (CMP-14).
 * - `targetLabel`/`targetPlaceholder`  label + placeholder for the SECOND field
 *                      (default "Target"; same authored-input-wins rule).
 * - `showTarget`       render the second field (default true). Lessons whose input
 *                      is a single value (e.g. recursion) hide it. LABELS ONLY —
 *                      the client still composes the same raw string parseInput
 *                      already accepts, so per-algorithm contracts are unchanged.
 *
 * Architecture (M3 §1, TD-1/TD-2): the `.astro` FRONTMATTER runs only at build
 * time and ships no client JS, so it `await`s the SAME lazy registry thunks the
 * client uses, runs the algorithm once, and calls the renderer's DOM-free
 * `renderStatic(step0)` to emit a correct still + per-algorithm aria copy — zero
 * client cost and no duplicated geometry. The bundled `<script>` lazily imports
 * only this lesson's two chunks on first intersection, mounts the live renderer
 * in place of the still, and wires the controls. An `AbortController` tears every
 * listener/timer/observer down on `astro:before-swap` (TD-4) so M4 View
 * Transitions never leak. The `<noscript>` kill-switch hides the controls and
 * reveals the "enable JS" note.
 *
 * M7.2 (Player v2) adds — all on the same pipeline, the trace is still computed
 * once and only indexed into (§11):
 * - a BUILD-TIME legend of the highlight kinds this lesson's trace really uses
 *   (`core/legend`), so the colours stop being a private code;
 * - runtime bound state as `aria-disabled` instead of `disabled` (A11Y-1), so a
 *   focused button never dies under the keyboard user and the island's ←/→
 *   shortcuts keep working. The REAL `disabled` attribute survives in SSR only,
 *   where it still has to block a pre-hydration submit;
 * - lifecycle states that are told apart: loading (the bar still dimmed by the
 *   SSR `disabled` recipe, plus a "…" counter and `aria-busy` — both set by JS
 *   ONLY, so a JS-off page is never "busy") versus failed (`data-viz-failed`
 *   hides the controls, and the message says what the reader can still do);
 * - an aria-live policy for autoplay above 1× (A11Y-6, spec §10's M7 exception);
 * - custom-input recovery: helper text disclosing the real per-algorithm cap, and
 *   a "Restore example" control that puts the authored run back (CMP-14);
 * - RSP-2's mobile legibility floor: the canvas scrolls rather than shrinking the
 *   drawing below readable, and becomes a keyboard-reachable scroll region for
 *   exactly as long as it actually overflows.
 *
 * M8.2 adds PREDICT-THE-STEP — an opt-in retrieval mode, not a second player.
 * It consumes the SAME precomputed trace (spec §11.3: no mechanic may add
 * algorithm logic to the island, the Player, or a renderer) by asking the
 * algorithm's optional `predictStep(trace, i, input)` what is worth predicting
 * at the current index:
 * - the toggle is rendered ONLY where the build proved this lesson's authored
 *   run really asks something, and it is NEVER persisted — no storage key
 *   exists for it, so "per page visit" is a property of the code rather than a
 *   promise about it (spec §6);
 * - while it is on, auto-play and the scrubber are `aria-disabled` with a
 *   stated reason (A11Y-1 again: never the `disabled` attribute), Step forward
 *   stays live as an explicit no-penalty SKIP, and answering advances one step
 *   so the authored explanation of that step IS the feedback — a right and a
 *   wrong answer get the same immediate elaboration, which is the whole
 *   mechanism (errorful generation with elaborated feedback);
 * - the session line counts ACTIVITY ("7 answered · 2 skipped") and never a
 *   score, ratio or percentage: an accuracy display during a learning act makes
 *   beginners protect a number instead of attempting hard predictions
 *   (`docs/m8-gamification.md`, calm invariants). The ≥5-answers-at-≥80% bar
 *   that feeds the mastery ladder is therefore computed silently and written
 *   through `recordPass()`, which owns the 3-day Mastered gate — predict adds no
 *   promotion rule of its own and stores no attempt history.
 * The question strip is INJECTED at runtime rather than server-rendered:
 * predict needs JS anyway, and `.viz-frame` is outside the `<noscript>`
 * kill-switch's reach (it only targets `.viz-controls`), so a JS-off page must
 * never grow one.
 *
 * M7.3 changes only how this island is PAINTED — no markup, no script, no new
 * token, no new key:
 * - the elevation inversion (VD-8) flipped what `--bg` and `--surface` mean, so
 *   every consumer of either was re-audited against the level model `global.css`
 *   now owns. The verdict per consumer, and the ratio behind it, is the
 *   "Elevation audit" comment at the top of the stylesheet;
 * - forced-colors hardening (HCM-1): in Windows High Contrast the UA overrides
 *   author fill and stroke, so the eight `--hl-*` cell/node states collapse into
 *   one system colour — in the one module the site exists for. The block at the
 *   end of the stylesheet re-encodes every state on the channels forced colours
 *   preserve (stroke width, dash pattern, and the marker glyphs, which are text).
 */
import { algorithms, renderers } from './registry';
import { collectLegend } from './core/legend';
import {
  inputHelpText,
  probeListCap,
  splitAuthoredInput,
} from './core/input-hint';
import type { Algorithm, RendererModule, Step } from './core/types';

interface Props {
  algorithm: string;
  renderer: string;
  input?: string;
  allowCustomInput?: boolean;
  showMetrics?: boolean;
  inputLabel?: string;
  inputPlaceholder?: string;
  targetLabel?: string;
  targetPlaceholder?: string;
  showTarget?: boolean;
}

const {
  algorithm,
  renderer,
  input,
  allowCustomInput = true,
  showMetrics = true,
  // Defaults keep every array/search/sort lesson (incl. binary-search) unchanged.
  inputLabel = 'Array',
  // No literal placeholder defaults (M7.2 CMP-14): with an authored `input` the
  // placeholders are DERIVED from it below, so these two only cover the viz that
  // has no authored input to derive from.
  inputPlaceholder,
  targetLabel = 'Target',
  targetPlaceholder,
  showTarget = true,
} = Astro.props;

// Unique id base so multiple visualizers on one page don't collide.
const uid = `viz-${Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 8)}`;

// --- Build-time still (architecture §1): resolve the lazy chunks, run the algo,
// render step 0 to a DOM-free SVG string. Unknown ids or malformed authored
// input FAIL THE BUILD here, which is a better DX than M2's runtime fallback. ---
type AnyAlgoMap = Record<string, () => Promise<Algorithm<unknown, unknown>>>;
type AnyRendererMap = Record<string, () => Promise<RendererModule<unknown>>>;
const algo = await (algorithms as AnyAlgoMap)[algorithm]?.();
const rmod = await (renderers as AnyRendererMap)[renderer]?.();
if (!algo || !rmod) {
  throw new Error(
    `Unknown viz: algorithm="${algorithm}" renderer="${renderer}"`,
  );
}

const parsed = input ? algo.parseInput(input) : algo.defaultInput();
if (parsed && typeof parsed === 'object' && 'error' in parsed) {
  throw new Error(
    `Authored <Visualizer> input rejected: ${(parsed as { error: string }).error}`,
  );
}
const trace = algo.run(parsed);
const step0 = trace[0] as Step<unknown> | undefined;
if (!step0)
  throw new Error(`Algorithm "${algorithm}" produced an empty trace.`);

const label = algo.label;
const still = rmod.renderStatic(step0, { title: label, idBase: uid });
const step0Text = step0.explanation;

// Step-0 metric pills, rendered at BUILD time. An empty `.viz-metrics` measures
// 0px and grew ~36px the moment the island hydrated, shifting the document under
// any in-flight smooth scroll (MOT-1) so anchors landed short; shipping the same
// pills the client's renderMetrics() builds makes hydration layout-neutral — and
// makes the counts readable with JS off. Same label + `<b>` value shape as that
// function — keep the two in sync — and the same rendered box: `.viz-pill` is a
// flex container, so the template's indentation collapses to nothing (a
// whitespace-only anonymous flex item is not rendered) and both paths lay out as
// "label item + gap + <b> item".
const metricPills = Object.entries(step0.metrics ?? {}).map(([key, value]) => ({
  label: `${key[0]!.toUpperCase()}${key.slice(1)} `,
  value: String(value),
}));

// Auto-legend (M7.2): the trace above is the real one the Player will index
// into, so scanning it for the highlight kinds it actually emits produces a
// legend that cannot drift from the drawing — and costs zero client JS. Kinds
// this lesson never uses are never shown.
const legend = collectLegend(trace);

// --- Predict-the-Step (M8.2) ---
// `predictStep` is OPTIONAL on the Algorithm contract (spec §11.2), so the
// toggle exists only where this lesson's algorithm ships one AND the authored
// run really has something to ask about — a mode control that can never ask a
// question is worse than no control at all. Both facts are settled HERE, at
// build time, from the same trace the Player will index into: predict CONSUMES
// the precomputed trace and never forks the pipeline (spec §11.3).
//
// The scan doubles as the contract's enforcement point. A question with fewer
// than 2 or more than 4 choices, or a `correctIndex` outside them, fails the
// BUILD rather than rendering a one-button "prediction" (the design's floor
// guard for BFS's near-path graph, where the frontier often holds a single id)
// or a strip whose answer cannot be picked.
let predictQuestions = 0;
if (algo.predictStep) {
  for (let i = 0; i < trace.length; i += 1) {
    const question = algo.predictStep(trace, i, parsed);
    if (!question) continue;
    const size = question.choices.length;
    if (
      size < 2 ||
      size > 4 ||
      !Number.isInteger(question.correctIndex) ||
      question.correctIndex < 0 ||
      question.correctIndex >= size
    ) {
      throw new Error(
        `predictStep("${algorithm}") returned an out-of-contract question at step ${i}: choices must number 2–4 with correctIndex inside them (spec §11.2). Got ${size} choices, correctIndex ${question.correctIndex}.`,
      );
    }
    predictQuestions += 1;
  }
}
const showPredict = predictQuestions > 0;

// The lesson a passed predict session credits, resolved at BUILD time from the
// page URL — `/learn/{slug}` and nothing else. This island also renders on
// /about and in the dev gallery, where there is no lesson to credit and the
// attribute is simply absent; `recordPass` is then never called, which is the
// honest outcome (the prediction practice still works). Build-time rather than a
// client-side path parse: the answer is static per page, costs no bytes, and
// cannot disagree with the route after a View Transition. The `.html`/trailing-
// slash trim keeps it true under either `build.format`.
const routePath = Astro.url.pathname
  .replace(/(?:\/index)?\.html$/, '')
  .replace(/\/$/, '');
const lessonSlug = /^\/learn\/([^/]+)$/.exec(routePath)?.[1] ?? '';

// CMP-14: the fields advertised a generic `[1,3,5,7,9,11]` while the canvas
// showed the AUTHORED example, so "Run" on a half-edited field produced
// something unrelated to the picture. Derive both placeholders from the authored
// input, split on the same `target=` separator the submit handler composes (the
// PARSED object can't be used — it is per-algorithm and typed `unknown` here).
// The DERIVED value wins over the prop: a hand-written placeholder that
// disagrees with the drawing is the exact defect this exists to remove, and
// several lessons pass one naming a different array. The props survive for the
// only case derivation can't serve — a viz with no `input`, seeded from
// `defaultInput()`, whose parsed shape this generic file cannot stringify — with
// the old literals behind them as the last resort.
const authored = splitAuthoredInput(input ?? '');
const arrayPlaceholder = authored.input || inputPlaceholder || '[1,3,5,7,9,11]';
const targetHint = authored.target || targetPlaceholder || '7';

// Helper text (INP-*): format + the REAL cap. Each algorithm enforces its own
// (30 array items, 15 heap values, 12 stack items…), so the number is measured
// against this algorithm's own parseInput at build time rather than hardcoded —
// a literal "up to 30" would be wrong on six lessons. `null` (no bracketed list
// to vary, or an uncapped parser) simply drops the sentence.
const itemCap = probeListCap(input ?? '', (raw) => algo.parseInput(raw));
const helpText = inputHelpText({ example: arrayPlaceholder, cap: itemCap });

const explainId = `${uid}-explain`;
const predictNoteId = `${uid}-predict-note`;
const errId = `${uid}-err`;
const helpId = `${uid}-help`;
const stepId = `${uid}-step`;
const speedId = `${uid}-speed`;
const arrayId = `${uid}-array`;
const targetId = `${uid}-target`;
---

<section
  class="viz"
  aria-label={`${label} visualization`}
  data-viz
  data-algorithm={algorithm}
  data-renderer={renderer}
  data-input={input ?? ''}
  data-label={label}
  data-show-metrics={showMetrics ? 'true' : 'false'}
  data-target-label={showTarget ? targetLabel : ''}
  data-slug={showPredict && lessonSlug ? lessonSlug : undefined}
>
  <figure class="viz-frame">
    {
      /* Build-time static still (renderStatic step 0); upgraded in place on hydrate.
         tabindex="-1" is the click-then-Space focus target (the pointerdown
         handler focuses it, so the island's shortcuts work after a click on the
         drawing). It is NOT keyboard-reachable at -1; the script promotes it to
         tabindex="0" + role="group" + a name for exactly as long as the drawing
         actually overflows its box (RSP-2), because a scroll container that the
         keyboard cannot reach would be a 2.1.1 failure. The measurement — and
         therefore the whole scroll affordance — is JS-only, so the JS-off page
         keeps today's scale-to-fit still and never gets an unreachable scroller. */
    }
    <div class="viz-canvas" data-viz-canvas tabindex="-1" set:html={still} />

    {
      /* Auto-legend (build time): one pill per highlight kind this trace really
         uses. The swatch repeats the renderer's own token + tint, the glyph is
         the character the renderer draws, and the word is the plain-language
         name — so colour is never the only signal here either. `active` and
         `frontier` have no character marker; frontier's dashed swatch border
         carries its non-colour cue. */
    }
    {
      legend.length > 0 && (
        <ul
          class="viz-legend"
          role="list"
          aria-label="What the highlights mean"
          data-viz-legend
        >
          {legend.map((item) => (
            <li class="viz-pill viz-legend__item">
              <span
                class="viz-legend__swatch"
                data-kind={item.kind}
                style={`--legend-hl: var(${item.token})`}
                aria-hidden="true"
              />
              {item.glyph && (
                <span class="viz-legend__glyph" aria-hidden="true">
                  {item.glyph}
                </span>
              )}
              <span>{item.word}</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )
    }

    {
      /* A11Y-2: exposed, not aria-hidden — the pills are a SIBLING of the
         aria-live explanation, so screen-reader users can read the counts on
         demand without the live region re-announcing them every step. */
    }
    {
      showMetrics && (
        <div class="viz-metrics" data-viz-metrics>
          {metricPills.map((pill) => (
            <span class="viz-pill">
              {pill.label}
              <b>{pill.value}</b>
            </span>
          ))}
        </div>
      )
    }

    <p
      class="viz-explain"
      id={explainId}
      aria-live="polite"
      aria-atomic="true"
      data-viz-explain
    >
      {step0Text}
    </p>
  </figure>

  <div class="viz-controls" data-viz-controls>
    {
      /* One row from 640px up — transport | scrubber (flex) | speed — and two
         rows below it (transport, then scrubber + speed). DOM order IS the
         visual order at every width, so the tab order never disagrees with the
         layout. Every button ships the real `disabled` attribute for the
         pre-hydration paint only; from hydration on, bound state is
         `aria-disabled` (A11Y-1). `title` mirrors each aria-label so the icons
         are hoverable-discoverable too. */
    }
    <div class="viz-control-bar">
      <div class="viz-transport">
        <button
          type="button"
          class="viz-btn"
          data-viz-reset
          aria-label="Reset to start"
          title="Reset to start"
          disabled
        >
          <svg
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            width="22"
            height="22"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="2"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
            aria-hidden="true"
          >
            <path d="M4 12a8 8 0 1 0 2.3-5.6"></path>
            <path d="M4 4v4h4"></path>
          </svg>
        </button>
        {
          /* Single chevrons, echoing the ←/→ keys that do the same thing. NOT
             the skip-to-end media glyphs they replace (they promised a jump to
             the end, which is the slider's job), and not mirrored-bar glyphs —
             explicitly rejected in the UX plan as an unestablished convention. */
        }
        <button
          type="button"
          class="viz-btn"
          data-viz-back
          aria-label="Step back"
          title="Step back"
          disabled
        >
          <svg
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            width="22"
            height="22"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="2.25"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
            aria-hidden="true"
          >
            <path d="M15 5l-7 7 7 7"></path>
          </svg>
        </button>
        {
          /* No aria-pressed (VIZ-9 / A11Y-7): the accessible name already
             carries the state (Play / Pause / Replay from start), and a toggle
             that both renames itself and reports pressed double-encodes it. */
        }
        <button
          type="button"
          class="viz-btn viz-btn--play"
          data-viz-play
          aria-label="Play"
          title="Play"
          disabled
        >
          <svg
            class="viz-play__play"
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            width="24"
            height="24"
            fill="currentColor"
            aria-hidden="true"
          >
            <path d="M8 5v14l11-7z"></path>
          </svg>
          <svg
            class="viz-play__pause"
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            width="24"
            height="24"
            fill="currentColor"
            aria-hidden="true"
          >
            <path d="M6 5h4v14H6z"></path>
            <path d="M14 5h4v14h-4z"></path>
          </svg>
        </button>
        <button
          type="button"
          class="viz-btn"
          data-viz-forward
          aria-label="Step forward"
          title="Step forward"
          disabled
        >
          <svg
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            width="22"
            height="22"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="2.25"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
            aria-hidden="true"
          >
            <path d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"></path>
          </svg>
        </button>

        {
          /* Predict-the-Step (M8.2), rendered only where the build proved this
             algorithm's predictor really asks something about this run. It is a
             MODE, not transport, so it takes the constant-label + `aria-pressed`
             pattern (M7.1 CMP-12: renaming the label AND reporting pressed
             double-encodes the state) and opts out of `.viz-btn`'s square 44px
             box. Last in the row, so the four icons keep the order readers
             already know and the tab order still runs left to right. `disabled`
             in SSR like every other control here — it can ask nothing until the
             algorithm chunk has loaded. */
        }
        {
          showPredict && (
            <button
              type="button"
              class="viz-btn viz-predict-toggle"
              data-viz-predict
              aria-pressed="false"
              title="Predict each step before it happens"
              disabled
            >
              <svg
                width="18"
                height="18"
                viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="2"
                aria-hidden="true"
              >
                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" />
                <circle
                  class="viz-predict-toggle__dot"
                  cx="12"
                  cy="12"
                  r="4.5"
                  fill="currentColor"
                  stroke="none"
                />
              </svg>
              <span>Predict</span>
            </button>
          )
        }
      </div>

      <div class="viz-scrub">
        <label for={stepId} class="viz-scrub__label">Step</label>
        <input
          type="range"
          id={stepId}
          class="viz-slider"
          min="0"
          max="0"
          step="1"
          value="0"
          disabled
          data-viz-slider
          aria-valuetext="Step 1 of 1"
        />
        <span class="viz-counter" data-viz-counter aria-hidden="true"
          >– / –</span
        >
      </div>

      <div class="viz-speed">
        <label for={speedId} class="viz-speed__label">Speed</label>
        <select id={speedId} class="viz-select" data-viz-speed disabled>
          <option value="0.5">0.5×</option>
          <option value="1" selected>1×</option>
          <option value="1.5">1.5×</option>
          <option value="2">2×</option>
          <option value="3">3×</option>
        </select>
      </div>
    </div>

    {
      /* The STATED REASON behind the two controls predict marks unavailable
         (task: `aria-disabled` + a reason, never the `disabled` attribute).
         Shipped hidden, revealed by the toggle, and named by `aria-describedby`
         on both of them while it is on: `aria-disabled` says "no", this says
         why — and names the control that still moves, because skipping is a
         first-class choice here, not a failure. */
    }
    {
      showPredict && (
        <p
          class="viz-predict-note"
          id={predictNoteId}
          data-viz-predict-note
          hidden
        >
          Auto-play and the step slider are off while Predict is on. Step
          forward moves on without answering.
        </p>
      )
    }

    {
      /* Discoverability: the island's shortcuts were previously undocumented in
         the UI. Inside `.viz-controls`, so the noscript kill-switch hides it
         with every other JS-only affordance. */
    }
    <p class="viz-keys">
      <kbd>Space</kbd> play/pause
      <span class="viz-keys__dot" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
      <kbd>←</kbd>/<kbd>→</kbd> step
    </p>

    {
      allowCustomInput && (
        <form class="viz-custom" data-viz-form novalidate>
          <p class="viz-custom__legend">Try your own input</p>
          <div class="viz-custom__fields">
            <div class="viz-field">
              <label for={arrayId}>{inputLabel}</label>
              {/* INP-1: these fields take code-shaped literals, so every
                  "helpful" mobile keyboard behaviour (autocorrect, leading
                  capital, spell squiggles) is noise here.
                  SPACE-SEPARATED describedby: the field owes BOTH descriptions
                  and the error one has to survive — overwriting the list with
                  just the error id (or the reverse) is how one of the two
                  silently stops being announced. Format first, then what went
                  wrong with it. */}
              <input
                type="text"
                id={arrayId}
                data-viz-array
                placeholder={arrayPlaceholder}
                aria-describedby={helpText ? `${helpId} ${errId}` : errId}
                spellcheck="false"
                autocapitalize="none"
                autocorrect="off"
                disabled
              />
            </div>
            {showTarget && (
              <div class="viz-field viz-field--target">
                <label for={targetId}>{targetLabel}</label>
                <input
                  type="text"
                  id={targetId}
                  data-viz-target
                  inputmode="numeric"
                  placeholder={targetHint}
                  aria-describedby={errId}
                  spellcheck="false"
                  autocapitalize="none"
                  autocorrect="off"
                  disabled
                />
              </div>
            )}
            <button type="submit" class="btn-secondary" data-viz-run disabled>
              Run
            </button>
            {/* Recovery (CMP-14): puts the lesson's own example back — the exact
                authored string, which the build already proved parses — and
                refills both fields with its REAL values, so an experiment is
                never a one-way door. Only offered when there IS an authored
                input; a viz seeded from defaultInput() has no string to restore
                and the control would lie about what it does. */}
            {input && (
              <button
                type="button"
                class="btn-secondary"
                data-viz-restore
                disabled
              >
                Restore example
              </button>
            )}
          </div>
          {/* Persistent format + cap disclosure (INP-*). Muted, not an error:
              it is the same text before and after a mistake, so the reader can
              act on it instead of guessing from a rejection. */}
          {helpText && (
            <p class="viz-custom__help" id={helpId}>
              {helpText}
            </p>
          )}
          {/* role="status" carries an implicit aria-live="polite"; the choice
              over role="alert" (A11Y-8) is therefore about politeness, not about
              announcing once: this error is the direct result of the reader's own
              submit, so it should queue behind whatever the AT is saying instead
              of interrupting it. showError also moves focus to the offending
              field, which re-announces the text via aria-describedby. */}
          <p class="viz-error" id={errId} role="status" data-viz-error hidden>
            <svg
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              width="16"
              height="16"
              fill="none"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="2"
              stroke-linecap="round"
              stroke-linejoin="round"
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
              <path d="M12 3 2 20h20z" />
              <path d="M12 10v4" />
              <path d="M12 17h.01" />
            </svg>
            <span data-viz-error-text />
          </p>
        </form>
      )
    }
  </div>

  <p class="viz-nojs-note">
    <svg
      viewBox="0 0 24 24"
      width="16"
      height="16"
      fill="none"
      stroke="currentColor"
      stroke-width="2"
      stroke-linecap="round"
      stroke-linejoin="round"
      aria-hidden="true"
    >
      <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"></circle>
      <path d="M12 16v-4"></path>
      <path d="M12 8h.01"></path>
    </svg>
    <span>Enable JavaScript to play, step, and run this on your own input.</span
    >
  </p>
</section>

<noscript>
  <style>
    /* Kill-switch (mirrors ThemeToggle): JS off → hide interactive controls,
       reveal the static-preview note. !important beats the bundled stylesheet. */
    [data-viz] .viz-controls {
      display: none !important;
    }
    [data-viz] .viz-nojs-note {
      display: flex !important;
    }
  </style>
</noscript>

<style is:global>
  /* ---- Elevation audit (M7.3 VD-8) ----
     The inversion made the page canvas the TINTED level (`--bg` #F8FAFC) and
     cards white (`--surface`), which changes the meaning of every fill in this
     file without changing a single token name. Each consumer below was re-read
     against the level model in `global.css` (0 canvas · 1 resting surface · 2
     raised · -1 sunken well) and either moved or deliberately kept, with the
     ratio that keeps it legal. Ratios are WCAG 2.1, computed from the token hex
     values, quoted light / dark.

       .viz-frame ................ level 1, unchanged token. It is now the light
         surface rather than the grey one, and its resting `--shadow-1` finally
         reads against a tinted page.
       .viz-cell__rect,
       .viz-node__circle ......... level 1, KEPT at `--surface` on purpose. Cell
         and frame have always shared this token (they were #F6F7F9 on #F6F7F9
         before), so the inversion changed nothing about their relationship: the
         boundary is, and always was, the 1.5px `--border-strong` stroke —
         4.76:1 / 3.07:1, both >= the 3:1 that WCAG 1.4.11 asks of it, and the
         light figure IMPROVED with the inversion (4.55:1 before). `--surface-
         sunken` was measured as the alternative (stroke 4.23:1 / 3.72:1, plus a
         1.12:1 / 1.21:1 fill delta against the frame) and rejected: three other
         places restate this exact recipe against `--surface` — the guard test
         `tests/unit/tokens-contrast.test.ts`, the home hero's `renderStatic()`
         still and the 404 still — and the two stills exist to show readers the
         real product, so the lesson must not paint its cells differently.
       highlight `color-mix()` fills . unchanged, and for the same reason: the
         tints mix over `--surface`. Each `--hl-*` stroke on its own tint is
         >= 3.92:1 / 4.20:1 (1.4.11 wants 3:1), `--text` on those tints is
         >= 13.87:1 / 7.95:1, and the marker glyphs — text, so they owe 4.5:1 —
         are >= 5.02:1 / 5.38:1 on the frame they are drawn over.
       .viz-weight-pill .......... level 1, kept. It MASKS the frame behind an
         edge weight, so being the frame's own colour is its whole job.
       .viz-pill ................. `--bg` -> `--surface-sunken`. Metric and
         legend chips are the only consumers here that sit INSIDE the frame, so
         the plan's blanket "move to --surface" would have painted them white on
         white; their `--border` keyline is just 1.22:1 there, so the fill has to
         carry the shape. A sunken chip on a level-1 card is 1.12:1 / 1.21:1 —
         faint by design, and the readable part clears AA either way
         (`--text-muted` 6.74:1 / 6.91:1, the `<b>` value 15.87:1 / 14.36:1).
       .viz-btn, .viz-select,
       .viz-field input,
       .viz-keys kbd ............. `--bg` -> `--surface`. These sit OUTSIDE the
         frame, on the page canvas, where level 1 is the raised reading — the
         move `.btn-secondary` already made. `--border-strong` is 4.55:1 on the
         canvas and 4.76:1 on their own fill; a sunken fill would have been
         1.06:1 against the canvas, i.e. gone.
       .viz-btn[aria-disabled] ... `--surface` -> `--bg`. The bound state works
         by flattening into the background, so it has to follow the background.
         `--text-muted` on it is 7.24:1 / 7.30:1 before the shared 0.5 opacity.
       .viz-slider thumb ring .... `--surface` -> `--bg`. The ring punches the
         thumb out of what is behind the slider, which is the canvas; against
         the brand-filled track it reads at 6.01:1 / 6.28:1. */

  /* ---- Region + frame ---- */
  .viz {
    margin-block: var(--space-8);
  }

  /* RSP-2: full-bleed below the md breakpoint. The drawing is the widest thing
     on a lesson page and the shell's gutters (`main`'s `px-4`) were costing it
     2rem of a 390px screen; the negative margin is EXACTLY that gutter, so the
     document still never scrolls sideways. Side borders and the card radius go
     with it — a rounded card that touches both edges reads as a rendering bug. */
  .viz-frame {
    margin: 0 calc(var(--space-4) * -1);
    background-color: var(--surface);
    border-block: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 0;
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-1);
    padding: var(--space-4);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .viz-frame {
      margin-inline: 0;
      border-inline: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: var(--radius-card);
      padding: var(--space-6);
    }
  }

  /* RSP-2 legibility floor. At the permitted n = 30 the array viewBox is ~1870
     units wide, so `width: 100%` scaled the 20px value text down to ~3.5px on a
     phone. The drawing now keeps at least 75% of its designed size and the box
     scrolls instead — chosen over the intrinsic 100% because a 6-cell default
     would otherwise start scrolling on a 390px screen it currently fits.
     `--viz-natural-w` is written by the script from the live `viewBox` (never at
     build time): with JS off there is no measurement, no scroll container, and
     therefore no unreachable-scroller keyboard failure — and JS-off only ever
     shows the small authored input anyway. */
  .viz-canvas {
    margin-block-end: var(--space-4);
    overflow-x: auto;
    overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
  }

  .viz-canvas > svg {
    min-width: calc(var(--viz-natural-w, 0px) * 0.75);
  }

  /* ---- SVG cells + markers (shared by static still and hydrated renderer) ---- */
  .viz-cell__rect {
    fill: var(--surface);
    stroke: var(--border-strong);
    stroke-width: 1.5;
    transition:
      fill var(--duration-base) var(--ease-standard),
      stroke var(--duration-base) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .viz-cell {
    transition: transform var(--duration-base) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .viz-cell__value {
    fill: var(--text);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 20px;
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  .viz-cell__index {
    fill: var(--text-muted);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 12px;
  }

  .viz-cell.is-range .viz-cell__rect {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hl-active) 15%, var(--surface));
  }

  .viz-cell.is-active {
    transform: translateY(-3px);
  }

  .viz-cell.is-active .viz-cell__rect {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hl-active) 15%, var(--surface));
    stroke: var(--hl-active);
    stroke-width: 3;
  }

  .viz-cell.is-found .viz-cell__rect {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hl-found) 18%, var(--surface));
    stroke: var(--hl-found);
    stroke-width: 3;
  }

  .viz-cell.is-eliminated {
    opacity: 0.42;
  }

  .viz-range-bar {
    stroke: var(--hl-active);
    stroke-width: 3;
    stroke-linecap: round;
  }

  .viz-marker {
    fill: var(--text-muted);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  .viz-mid-label {
    fill: var(--text);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: var(--weight-bold);
  }

  .viz-found-mark {
    fill: var(--hl-found);
    font-size: 18px;
    font-weight: var(--weight-bold);
  }

  /* ---- M3 renderer suite: extra highlight kinds for rect cells (design §2.2) ---- */
  .viz-cell.is-compare .viz-cell__rect {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hl-compare) 15%, var(--surface));
    stroke: var(--hl-compare);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }

  .viz-cell.is-swap .viz-cell__rect {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hl-swap) 15%, var(--surface));
    stroke: var(--hl-swap);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }

  .viz-cell.is-visited .viz-cell__rect {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hl-visited) 15%, var(--surface));
    stroke: var(--hl-visited);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }

  .viz-cell.is-frontier .viz-cell__rect {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hl-frontier) 15%, var(--surface));
    stroke: var(--hl-frontier);
    stroke-width: 2;
    stroke-dasharray: 4 3;
  }

  .viz-cell.is-insert .viz-cell__rect {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hl-found) 18%, var(--surface));
    stroke: var(--hl-found);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }

  .viz-cell.is-delete .viz-cell__rect {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hl-swap) 15%, var(--surface));
    stroke: var(--hl-swap);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }

  /* is-pointer is a caret-only cue — no rect treatment (design §1). */

  /* ---- Node primitive (tree / heap-tree / graph) ---- */
  .viz-node {
    transition: transform var(--duration-base) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .viz-node__circle {
    fill: var(--surface);
    stroke: var(--border-strong);
    stroke-width: 1.5;
    transition:
      fill var(--duration-base) var(--ease-standard),
      stroke var(--duration-base) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .viz-node__value {
    fill: var(--text);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  .viz-node__label {
    fill: var(--text-muted);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 12px;
  }

  .viz-node.is-range .viz-node__circle {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hl-active) 15%, var(--surface));
  }

  .viz-node.is-active {
    transform: translateY(-3px);
  }

  .viz-node.is-active .viz-node__circle {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hl-active) 15%, var(--surface));
    stroke: var(--hl-active);
    stroke-width: 3;
  }

  .viz-node.is-compare .viz-node__circle {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hl-compare) 15%, var(--surface));
    stroke: var(--hl-compare);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }

  .viz-node.is-swap .viz-node__circle {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hl-swap) 15%, var(--surface));
    stroke: var(--hl-swap);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }

  .viz-node.is-visited .viz-node__circle {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hl-visited) 15%, var(--surface));
    stroke: var(--hl-visited);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }

  .viz-node.is-frontier .viz-node__circle {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hl-frontier) 15%, var(--surface));
    stroke: var(--hl-frontier);
    stroke-width: 2;
    stroke-dasharray: 4 3;
  }

  .viz-node.is-found .viz-node__circle {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hl-found) 18%, var(--surface));
    stroke: var(--hl-found);
    stroke-width: 3;
  }

  .viz-node.is-insert .viz-node__circle {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hl-found) 18%, var(--surface));
    stroke: var(--hl-found);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }

  .viz-node.is-delete .viz-node__circle {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hl-swap) 15%, var(--surface));
    stroke: var(--hl-swap);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }

  .viz-node.is-eliminated {
    opacity: 0.42;
  }

  /* ---- Edges / arrows / connectors ---- */
  .viz-edge {
    stroke: var(--border-strong);
    stroke-width: 1.5;
    fill: none;
  }

  .viz-edge.is-path {
    stroke: var(--hl-active);
    stroke-width: 2.5;
    stroke-dasharray: 5 4;
  }

  .viz-arrow {
    fill: var(--border-strong);
    stroke: none;
  }

  .viz-arrow.is-path {
    fill: var(--hl-active);
  }

  .viz-tie {
    stroke: var(--hl-compare);
    stroke-width: 1.5;
    stroke-dasharray: 4 3;
    fill: none;
  }

  .viz-tether {
    stroke-width: 1;
    stroke-dasharray: 3 3;
    opacity: 0.6;
    fill: none;
  }

  .viz-divider {
    stroke: var(--border);
    stroke-width: 1;
  }

  /* ---- Marker glyphs (non-color layer, design §2.4) ---- */
  .viz-caret {
    fill: var(--text);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: var(--weight-bold);
  }

  .viz-insert-mark {
    fill: var(--hl-found);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: var(--weight-bold);
  }

  .viz-delete-mark {
    fill: var(--hl-swap);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: var(--weight-bold);
  }

  .viz-swap-mark {
    fill: var(--hl-swap);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: var(--weight-bold);
  }

  .viz-badge {
    fill: var(--hl-visited);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: var(--weight-bold);
  }

  .viz-strike {
    stroke: var(--hl-swap);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }

  .viz-null {
    fill: var(--text-muted);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 18px;
  }

  /* ---- Call-stack frame text ---- */
  .viz-frame-label {
    fill: var(--text);
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  .viz-frame-meta {
    fill: var(--text-muted);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 12px;
  }

  /* ---- Chart (Big-O growth curves, design §2b.8) ---- */
  .viz-axis {
    stroke: var(--border-strong);
    stroke-width: 1;
    fill: none;
  }

  .viz-gridline {
    stroke: var(--border);
    stroke-width: 1;
  }

  .viz-curve {
    stroke: var(--text-muted);
    stroke-width: 2.5;
    fill: none;
  }

  .viz-curve.is-emph {
    stroke: var(--hl-active);
    stroke-width: 3;
  }

  .viz-curve.is-emph-compare {
    stroke: var(--hl-compare);
    stroke-width: 3;
  }

  .viz-curve-label {
    fill: var(--text);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    font-weight: var(--weight-bold);
  }

  .viz-axis-label {
    fill: var(--text-muted);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
  }

  .viz-weight-pill {
    fill: var(--surface);
    stroke: var(--border);
    stroke-width: 1;
  }

  .viz-weight {
    fill: var(--text);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 12px;
  }

  /* ---- Metrics ---- */
  .viz-metrics {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }

  /* A chip INSIDE the level-1 frame, so it is a sunken well, not a raised
     control (elevation audit above): on a white card `--surface` would be
     invisible and `--bg` nearly so. */
  .viz-pill {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    background-color: var(--surface-sunken);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-3);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .viz-pill b {
    color: var(--text);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  /* ---- Legend (build-time; one pill per kind the trace really uses) ---- */
  .viz-legend {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* The swatch repeats the cell recipe exactly — the same token, the same 15%
     tint over the same --surface backdrop — so it reads as a sample of the
     drawing rather than a decorative dot. --legend-hl is set inline from
     HIGHLIGHTS[kind].token, which keeps the token mapping in ONE place. It
     survives the pill's move to a sunken fill (M7.3): the tint still samples the
     cell, and the 2px --hl-* border is >= 4.46:1 / 6.51:1 against the chip. */
  .viz-legend__swatch {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    width: 0.875rem;
    height: 0.875rem;
    border: 2px solid var(--legend-hl);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--legend-hl) 15%, var(--surface));
  }

  /* frontier is drawn as a DASHED ring and has no character marker, so the dash
     is its non-colour cue here too (design §1: colour is never the only signal). */
  .viz-legend__swatch[data-kind='frontier'] {
    border-style: dashed;
  }

  .viz-legend__glyph {
    color: var(--text);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-weight: var(--weight-bold);
    line-height: 1;
  }

  /* ---- Explanation ---- */
  .viz-explain {
    margin: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-top: var(--space-3);
    /* CMP-6: reserve two full lines so a one→two line explanation can't shove
       the controls down mid-step. box-sizing is border-box (Tailwind preflight),
       so the reservation has to include this box's own padding and border. */
    min-height: calc(
      2 * var(--leading-relaxed) * 1em + var(--space-3) + var(--space-px)
    );
    color: var(--text);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /* ---- Predict-the-Step strip (M8.2) ----
     Injected above the explanation at RUNTIME only: the noscript kill-switch
     covers `.viz-controls`, not `.viz-frame`, so a server-rendered strip would
     survive with JS off and offer a question nothing could grade. Every rule
     below therefore only ever paints something a script created.

     Each `[hidden]` companion rule is load-bearing, not belt-and-braces: this
     stylesheet is UNLAYERED and Tailwind's preflight (which hides `[hidden]`)
     is in `@layer base`, so an unlayered `display` here would beat the
     attribute — the same reason `.viz-error[hidden]` exists further down. */
  .viz-predict {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }

  .viz-predict[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  .viz-predict__prompt {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  /* The steps a predictor has nothing to ask about (no dequeue next, the trace
     has ended) say so plainly and quietly — no nudge, no scolding, and the
     transport still does exactly what it always did. */
  .viz-predict[data-idle] .viz-predict__prompt {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-weight: var(--weight-normal);
  }

  .viz-predict__choices {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-2);
  }

  .viz-predict__choices[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  /* `.btn-secondary` carries the fill, the 44px target and all five states; only
     the flex behaviour is added here, so up to four labels share the row on a
     desktop and wrap instead of overflowing the frame on a phone. */
  .viz-predict__choice {
    flex: 1 1 auto;
  }

  /* ACTIVITY, never accuracy. This line may not grow a ratio, a percentage or a
     score (`docs/m8-gamification.md` calm invariants): a scoreboard during a
     learning act makes beginners protect the number instead of attempting the
     hard predictions the mechanic exists to elicit. It is a `.viz-pill`, so it
     inherits the sunken-chip recipe every other in-frame chip uses. */
  .viz-predict__activity {
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
  }

  .viz-predict__activity[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  /* The verdict prefix, written into the EXISTING live region together with the
     step explanation it belongs to. Deliberately not coloured: the glyph and the
     word carry it (§12 — never colour alone), painting "wrong" in
     `--accent-warn` would loss-frame the errorful first attempt this mechanic
     wants, and the six `--hl-*` roles stay reserved for the drawing
     (design-tokens-m1.md Decision 3). `data-verdict` is a test hook, not a paint
     hook — which is also why forced colours need no rule here. */
  .viz-verdict {
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  .viz-verdict__glyph {
    margin-inline-end: var(--space-1);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
  }

  /* ---- Control bar ----
     Two rows on a phone (transport, then scrubber + speed) collapsing to ONE row
     from 640px up: transport | scrubber (takes the slack) | speed. Named areas
     rather than source order tricks, so the DOM order the keyboard follows is
     the order the eye follows at both sizes. */
  .viz-control-bar {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    grid-template-areas:
      'transport transport'
      'scrub speed';
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .viz-control-bar {
      grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
      grid-template-areas: 'transport scrub speed';
      column-gap: var(--space-4);
    }
  }

  .viz-scrub {
    grid-area: scrub;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .viz-scrub__label,
  .viz-speed__label {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* VD-4: the UA track sits at ~1.3:1 against the card, so both halves are
     token-styled here — `appearance: none` removes the native thumb along with
     the track, which is why each engine needs its own pair of rules. */
  .viz-slider {
    /* Elapsed fraction of the trace; rewritten once per step by onStep. */
    --viz-progress: 0%;
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 44px;
    appearance: none;
    background: transparent;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  /* The two-stop gradient IS the elapsed fill: no extra element, no per-frame JS. */
  .viz-slider::-webkit-slider-runnable-track {
    height: 6px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    background-image: linear-gradient(
      to right,
      var(--brand) var(--viz-progress),
      var(--border-strong) var(--viz-progress)
    );
  }

  .viz-slider::-moz-range-track {
    height: 6px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    background-image: linear-gradient(
      to right,
      var(--brand) var(--viz-progress),
      var(--border-strong) var(--viz-progress)
    );
  }

  /* Firefox has its own elapsed segment; the gradient is the single source of
     truth for the fill, so keep that segment out of the way. */
  .viz-slider::-moz-range-progress {
    background: transparent;
  }

  .viz-slider::-webkit-slider-thumb {
    -webkit-appearance: none;
    /* Preflight's `*` reset doesn't reach UA pseudo-elements — say it here so
       the border is inside the 18px and the -6px centring stays true. */
    box-sizing: border-box;
    width: 18px;
    height: 18px;
    margin-top: -6px; /* centre the thumb on the 6px track */
    /* The ring punches the thumb out of what is BEHIND the slider, which is the
       page canvas — `--bg` since the M7.3 inversion, not `--surface`. */
    border: 2px solid var(--bg);
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    background-color: var(--brand);
  }

  .viz-slider::-moz-range-thumb {
    box-sizing: border-box;
    width: 18px;
    height: 18px;
    border: 2px solid var(--bg);
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    background-color: var(--brand);
  }

  /* Forced colors drops author background-images, so VD-4's gradient track would
     leave the thumb floating on nothing — a rail the native track used to give
     us for free. Repaint both halves in system colors, which are deliberately
     token-free: the user's palette IS the contract here, and system colors are
     the one kind of author color forced-colors mode does not override (so no
     `forced-colors-adjust: none`, which would also opt the focus ring out). The
     elapsed fill can't survive without a background-image; the counter and
     aria-valuetext carry position. Each engine needs its own rule — one unknown
     pseudo-element in a selector list invalidates the whole rule. */
  @media (forced-colors: active) {
    .viz-slider::-webkit-slider-runnable-track {
      background-image: none;
      background-color: ButtonBorder;
    }

    .viz-slider::-moz-range-track {
      background-image: none;
      background-color: ButtonBorder;
    }

    .viz-slider::-webkit-slider-thumb {
      border-color: Canvas;
      background-color: Highlight;
    }

    .viz-slider::-moz-range-thumb {
      border-color: Canvas;
      background-color: Highlight;
    }
  }

  .viz-counter {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    min-width: 5ch;
    text-align: right;
  }

  .viz-transport {
    grid-area: transport;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .viz-transport {
      justify-content: flex-start;
    }
  }

  /* Transport buttons now have a resting SURFACE, in the same idiom as
     `.btn-secondary` (surface + 1px --border-strong + --radius-control): four
     ghost icons on a card gave the reader nothing to aim at and made the
     disabled delta a colour shift of a colour they had never seen enabled.
     They sit on the page canvas, so `--surface` is the RAISED level here
     (M7.3 VD-8) — the same move `.btn-secondary` made. */
  .viz-btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 44px;
    height: 44px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius-control);
    background-color: var(--surface);
    color: var(--text);
    cursor: pointer;
    transition:
      border-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard),
      background-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  /* Five-state recipe (CMP-8), matching `.btn-secondary` exactly: hover moves
     the fill as well as the border, because on a near-white canvas a 1px border
     colour change on its own reads as a rendering artefact; :active sinks to the
     well level — a fill move, never a transform, since motion in this component
     belongs to the drawing. :focus-visible is deliberately absent: the global
     ring owns it. --text is 15.97:1 / 11.82:1 on --brand-soft and 15.87:1 /
     14.36:1 on --surface-sunken. */
  .viz-btn:not(:disabled):not([aria-disabled='true']):hover {
    border-color: var(--brand);
    background-color: var(--brand-soft);
  }

  .viz-btn:not(:disabled):not([aria-disabled='true']):active {
    border-color: var(--brand);
    background-color: var(--surface-sunken);
  }

  /* Play keeps the brand fill but drops 56px → 44px: it now sits in one row with
     four other 44px controls, and 44px is still the AA target size. */
  .viz-btn--play {
    border-color: var(--brand);
    background-color: var(--brand);
    color: var(--brand-contrast);
  }

  /* The brand family's own shades, like `.btn-primary`: --brand-hover darkens in
     light and lightens in dark, and :active takes one more step in whichever
     direction the theme travels. --brand-contrast stays >= 7.90:1 on both. */
  .viz-btn--play:not(:disabled):not([aria-disabled='true']):hover {
    color: var(--brand-contrast);
    background-color: var(--brand-hover);
  }

  .viz-btn--play:not(:disabled):not([aria-disabled='true']):active {
    color: var(--brand-contrast);
    background-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--brand-hover) 88%, var(--text));
  }

  /* Play/Pause glyph swap driven by data-playing on the button. */
  .viz-play__pause {
    display: none;
  }

  .viz-btn--play[data-playing] .viz-play__play {
    display: none;
  }

  .viz-btn--play[data-playing] .viz-play__pause {
    display: inline;
  }

  /* Predict toggle: a `.viz-btn` that is text-labelled, so it drops the square
     44px box for an auto width (the 44px MINIMUM survives — `.viz-btn`'s height
     is unchanged). */
  .viz-predict-toggle {
    width: auto;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    padding-inline: var(--space-3);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  /* ON, in three signals, and the first is not a colour: the glyph's ring gains
     a DOT — a shape, so it survives forced colours and every form of colour
     blindness — while the border turns brand and an inset ring thickens it
     WITHOUT reflowing the row. It is PracticeCheck's chosen-grade recipe on
     purpose: the same 12% brand tint over the same `--surface` control fill, so
     "this is on" looks identical in both places and its measured `--text`
     contrast (14.25:1 light / 12.91:1 dark) carries over unchanged. The state
     itself is carried to assistive tech by `aria-pressed`, and the visible label
     stays the constant "Predict". */
  .viz-predict-toggle__dot {
    display: none;
  }

  .viz-predict-toggle[aria-pressed='true'] .viz-predict-toggle__dot {
    display: inline;
  }

  .viz-predict-toggle[aria-pressed='true'] {
    border-color: var(--brand);
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--brand);
    background-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--brand) 12%, transparent);
  }

  .viz-speed {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2);
  }

  .viz-select {
    min-height: 44px;
    background-color: var(--surface);
    color: var(--text);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius-control);
    padding-inline: var(--space-3);
  }

  /* ---- Custom input ---- */
  .viz-custom {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
    padding-top: var(--space-4);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .viz-custom__legend {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .viz-custom__fields {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    align-items: end;
  }

  .viz-field {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-1);
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 12rem;
  }

  .viz-field--target {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    min-width: 6rem;
    max-width: 8rem;
  }

  .viz-field label {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .viz-field input {
    min-height: 44px;
    background-color: var(--surface);
    color: var(--text);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius-control);
    padding-inline: var(--space-3);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
  }

  /* Persistent format/cap disclosure under the fields. */
  .viz-custom__help {
    margin: var(--space-2) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* ---- Disabled recipe (CMP-3) ---- */
  /* Every viz control ships `disabled` in SSR and is enabled on hydrate, so they
     all have to read identically in that first paint — one token, one rule,
     instead of the two ad-hoc opacities this replaces. */
  .viz-slider:disabled,
  .viz-slider[aria-disabled='true'],
  .viz-btn:disabled,
  .viz-btn[aria-disabled='true'],
  .viz-select:disabled,
  .viz-field input:disabled {
    opacity: var(--disabled-opacity);
    cursor: not-allowed;
  }

  /* A11Y-1: from hydration on, a transport button at a trace bound is
     aria-disabled rather than `disabled` — it keeps focus and its place in the
     tab order (a `disabled` element drops focus to <body>, taking the island's
     ←/→ shortcuts with it), while the Player clamps the action to a no-op. It
     must READ as unavailable all the same, so it shares the opacity recipe above
     AND flattens: the fill drops to whatever it is sitting on and the border
     softens, which is the obvious delta the old ghost buttons could not express.
     Post-inversion (M7.3) that backdrop is the page canvas, so this follows
     `--bg` — pointing it at `--surface` would now paint the bound state in the
     RESTING colour and erase the delta entirely. `--text-muted` on `--bg` is
     7.24:1 / 7.30:1 before the shared 0.5 opacity. */
  .viz-btn[aria-disabled='true'] {
    border-color: var(--border);
    background-color: var(--bg);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .viz-error {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    padding-left: var(--space-3);
    /* --accent-warn, never --hl-compare: the six --hl-* roles stay reserved for
       the visualization (design-tokens-m1.md Decision 3). Colour is not the only
       signal — the warning glyph, the keyline and the wording all carry it. */
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent-warn);
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  .viz-error[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  .viz-error svg {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-top: 2px;
  }

  /* The stated reason behind predict's two unavailable controls. Shipped hidden
     in SSR and revealed by the toggle; `--text-muted` on the page canvas is
     7.24:1 / 7.30:1. Sized like `.viz-keys`, because it is the same kind of
     thing: a quiet note about how the transport behaves right now. */
  .viz-predict-note {
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
  }

  /* ---- Keyboard hint (JS-only affordance, so it lives inside .viz-controls) ---- */
  .viz-keys {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-1);
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .viz-keys kbd {
    border: 1px solid var(--border-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    padding: 0 var(--space-1);
    /* A keycap is a raised thing; on the tinted canvas that is `--surface`. */
    background-color: var(--surface);
    color: var(--text);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
  }

  .viz-keys__dot {
    color: var(--border-strong);
  }

  /* ---- Lifecycle: loading vs failed (they used to be pixel-identical) ---- */
  /* Loading — flagged by JS only, never in SSR. The control bar's DIM is already
     correct without a rule here: every control still carries the SSR `disabled`
     attribute at this point, so the shared disabled recipe above is painting it,
     and stacking a second opacity on the block would both compound to ~0.25 and
     drag the bar's label text under 4.5:1 for the length of the fetch. What this
     state adds is the "…" counter (written by the script), `aria-busy`, and a
     pointer that says "not yet" rather than "not ever". */
  [data-viz-loading] .viz-controls {
    cursor: progress;
  }

  /* Failed — nothing here will ever work, so the controls go entirely rather
     than sitting there inert; the message carries the recovery copy. The legend
     and metrics go with them: both describe a drawing that is no longer there. */
  [data-viz-failed] .viz-controls,
  [data-viz-failed] .viz-legend,
  [data-viz-failed] .viz-metrics {
    display: none;
  }

  /* ---- JS-off note (revealed by the noscript kill-switch) ---- */
  .viz-nojs-note {
    display: none;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    padding-top: var(--space-3);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* Failed state (chunk load failure / unparseable authored input — defense in
     depth). Full-strength text, not muted: it is the only thing left in the box
     and it has to be read, not skimmed past. */
  .viz-unavailable {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: var(--space-3);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent-warn);
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  /* ---- Forced colors / Windows High Contrast (M7.3 HCM-1) ----
     Forced-colors mode overrides author `fill` and `stroke`, so the drawing's
     entire colour layer — eight `--hl-*` states, each a `color-mix()` fill plus
     a matching stroke — collapses into one system colour, and "current window",
     "comparing" and "found" stop being different pictures in the one module
     this site exists for. Everything below re-encodes that state on the channels
     forced colours DO preserve, and paints in system colours, which are the one
     kind of author colour this mode does not override (same contract as the
     slider rules further up: the reader's palette wins).

     `forced-color-adjust: none` is deliberately NOT used. The spec does sanction
     it for meaningful data-visualization colour, but it opts an element out per
     property group rather than per layer: keeping a pale tinted rect while the
     value text inside it is still forced to the reader's text colour is exactly
     how you get white-on-pale-tint, and opting the whole drawing out instead
     would discard the palette they deliberately chose. It is never applied to
     chrome or to the focus ring.

     THE MAPPING — extend it here, in this one place. Widths are ordinal
     (thicker = more important right now) and every dash pattern is unique:

       state        width  dasharray         glyph that survives as text
       resting       1.5   solid             —
       is-range      1.5   1 4  fine dots    range bar / underbar
       is-frontier   2     4 3  dashes       dashed ring (graph)
       is-compare    2     10 4 long dashes  dashed tie-line
       is-visited    2     1 3  dots         ✓ badge
       is-swap       2     2 2  short dashes ↔
       is-insert     2     10 3 2 3 dash-dot +
       is-delete     2     2 6  sparse dots  ✕ + strikethrough
       is-active     3     solid             caret label + the 3px lift
       is-found      4     solid             ✓

     Only the DELTAS are declared below; where the everyday rules already carry
     the right channel (is-active's 3px, is-frontier's `4 3`, every 2px state)
     they are left to the cascade, so tuning a state in one theme tunes it here
     too. Two states need no rule and are named so the next reader knows they
     were checked, not missed: `is-pointer` draws no rect treatment at all (it is
     a caret), and `is-eliminated` dims through `opacity`, which forced colours
     leave alone. The legend needs none either — each pill carries the glyph and
     the plain word, and frontier's dashed swatch is a border-STYLE. */
  @media (forced-colors: active) {
    /* Repaint the drawing in system colours. The `:is()` list ties the per-state
       fill rules at (0,3,0) and wins on source order; without it `fill: Canvas`
       would lose to every `color-mix()` tint and the UA's own substitution — one
       flat colour for fill and stroke alike — would be all that is left. */
    .viz-cell__rect,
    .viz-node__circle,
    .viz-weight-pill,
    .viz-cell:is(
        .is-range,
        .is-active,
        .is-found,
        .is-compare,
        .is-swap,
        .is-visited,
        .is-frontier,
        .is-insert,
        .is-delete
      )
      .viz-cell__rect,
    .viz-node:is(
        .is-range,
        .is-active,
        .is-found,
        .is-compare,
        .is-swap,
        .is-visited,
        .is-frontier,
        .is-insert,
        .is-delete
      )
      .viz-node__circle {
      fill: Canvas;
      stroke: CanvasText;
    }

    /* The glyph band and every label. Said explicitly rather than left to the
       UA: engines disagree about whether SVG `fill` is forced at all, and a
       `--hl-found` ✓ left standing on a black Canvas would be ~2:1. */
    .viz-cell__value,
    .viz-cell__index,
    .viz-node__value,
    .viz-node__label,
    .viz-marker,
    .viz-mid-label,
    .viz-caret,
    .viz-found-mark,
    .viz-insert-mark,
    .viz-delete-mark,
    .viz-swap-mark,
    .viz-badge,
    .viz-null,
    .viz-frame-label,
    .viz-frame-meta,
    .viz-weight,
    .viz-curve-label,
    .viz-axis-label,
    .viz-arrow,
    .viz-arrow.is-path {
      fill: CanvasText;
    }

    /* Line work. `is-path` keeps its own dash + 2.5px width, which is what tells
       a path edge from a plain one once both are CanvasText. */
    .viz-edge,
    .viz-edge.is-path,
    .viz-range-bar,
    .viz-tie,
    .viz-tether,
    .viz-divider,
    .viz-strike,
    .viz-axis,
    .viz-gridline,
    .viz-curve,
    .viz-curve.is-emph,
    .viz-curve.is-emph-compare {
      stroke: CanvasText;
    }

    /* The state vocabulary (table above). */
    .viz-cell.is-range .viz-cell__rect,
    .viz-node.is-range .viz-node__circle {
      stroke-dasharray: 1 4;
    }

    .viz-cell.is-compare .viz-cell__rect,
    .viz-node.is-compare .viz-node__circle {
      stroke-dasharray: 10 4;
    }

    .viz-cell.is-visited .viz-cell__rect,
    .viz-node.is-visited .viz-node__circle {
      stroke-dasharray: 1 3;
    }

    .viz-cell.is-swap .viz-cell__rect,
    .viz-node.is-swap .viz-node__circle {
      stroke-dasharray: 2 2;
    }

    .viz-cell.is-insert .viz-cell__rect,
    .viz-node.is-insert .viz-node__circle {
      stroke-dasharray: 10 3 2 3;
    }

    .viz-cell.is-delete .viz-cell__rect,
    .viz-node.is-delete .viz-node__circle {
      stroke-dasharray: 2 6;
    }

    /* Terminal state: the only 4px stroke anywhere on the canvas. */
    .viz-cell.is-found .viz-cell__rect,
    .viz-node.is-found .viz-node__circle {
      stroke-width: 4;
    }

    /* Chart: which curve the step is talking about is encoded in colour alone
       (`--hl-active` / `--hl-compare` against `--text-muted`). Widen the gap the
       emphasis already opens — is-emph keeps its 3px — and dash the comparison
       curve. Each curve is also named by its own `.viz-curve-label`. */
    .viz-curve {
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }

    .viz-curve.is-emph-compare {
      stroke-dasharray: 10 4;
    }

    /* Chrome, not drawing: once colours flatten, the bound transport state is
       carried by `--disabled-opacity` alone, and dimming is not a cue the reader
       asked for. `GrayText` is the system colour that means exactly
       "unavailable" and the icon inherits it through `currentColor`. A repaint
       in system colours, not an opt-out — the focus ring is untouched. */
    .viz-btn[aria-disabled='true'] {
      opacity: 1;
      border-color: GrayText;
      color: GrayText;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { normalizeSpeed, Player } from './core/player';
  import { errorField } from './core/error-field';
  import { splitAuthoredInput } from './core/input-hint';
  import { algorithms, renderers } from './registry';
  // The `viz:run` name and its payload shape (M8.3). Imported rather than
  // retyped: `src/lib/challenges.ts` owns the contract because its consumers do,
  // and a second string literal here is exactly how a dispatcher and a listener
  // drift apart. It costs nothing on a lesson with no trials — that module is
  // pure declarations importing only types, so everything but this one string is
  // tree-shaken out of the island's chunk.
  import { VIZ_RUN_EVENT } from '../lib/challenges';
  import type { VizRunDetail } from '../lib/challenges';
  import type {
    Algorithm,
    PredictQuestion,
    Renderer,
    RendererModule,
    Step,
    Trace,
  } from './core/types';

  /**
   * The optional Predict-the-Step grader (spec §11.2), with the `undefined` an
   * algorithm without one contributes stripped off — this island stores it
   * bound, or `null`, and never re-checks the option.
   */
  type Predictor = NonNullable<Algorithm<unknown, unknown>['predictStep']>;

  /**
   * What the strip says on a step the predictor declines to ask about — a
   * dequeue is not next, the trace has ended. Plain and final: no nudge, no
   * "try again", nothing that reads as the product asking for something. The
   * review queue is the only surface in this product that ever prompts.
   */
  const PREDICT_IDLE = 'No prediction at this step.';

  /**
   * SPEC-GAP: §6 (amended for M7.2) permits the PREFERENCE key `pref:viz-speed`
   * but says nothing about its shape or its scope. Stored as the plain
   * multiplier string and shared by every visualizer on the page: playback speed
   * is a property of the reader, not of one island, and a per-island key would
   * multiply storage writes by the number of visualizers on a lesson. It is a
   * preference, so the reset-progress control must never clear it.
   */
  const SPEED_KEY = 'pref:viz-speed';

  /**
   * Same-page sync channel for that one preference, mirroring CodeTabs'
   * `codetabs:lang`: N islands share ONE speed, so the island the reader touched
   * has to tell its siblings. Without it a lesson with two visualizers keeps the
   * second one running at the old rate behind a `<select>` still showing 1×,
   * which contradicts the key's own "a property of the reader" rationale above.
   */
  const SPEED_EVENT = 'viz:speed';

  /** Narrow a parseInput result to its error branch. */
  function isParseError(value: unknown): value is { error: string } {
    return (
      typeof value === 'object' &&
      value !== null &&
      'error' in value &&
      typeof (value as { error: unknown }).error === 'string'
    );
  }

  /**
   * Reads the persisted playback speed, clamped to the `<select>`'s own options.
   * Storage-safe: private mode / disabled storage throws on access, and a
   * preference is never worth breaking the player over, so it degrades to `null`
   * (= keep the 1× default).
   */
  function readStoredSpeed(): number | null {
    try {
      return normalizeSpeed(localStorage.getItem(SPEED_KEY));
    } catch {
      return null;
    }
  }

  /** Persists the playback speed. Silently gives up when storage is unavailable. */
  function storeSpeed(value: string): void {
    try {
      localStorage.setItem(SPEED_KEY, value);
    } catch {
      // Preference-only: a failed write must never surface to the reader.
    }
  }

  /** Per-root teardown, so `astro:before-swap` can dispose every island (TD-4). */
  const teardowns = new WeakMap<HTMLElement, () => void>();

  /** Sets up one Visualizer container: observe → import → mount → wire. */
  function setupViz(root: HTMLElement): void {
    if (root.dataset['vizInit'] === 'true') return;
    root.dataset['vizInit'] = 'true';

    const algoId = root.dataset['algorithm'] ?? '';
    const rendererId = root.dataset['renderer'] ?? '';
    const rawInput = root.dataset['input'] ?? '';
    const title = root.dataset['label'] ?? '';
    const showMetrics = root.dataset['showMetrics'] === 'true';
    // The SECOND field's rendered label ("Target", "Index", "Search key",
    // "Start node"); empty when that field isn't rendered. errorField reads it
    // so error attribution follows the actual UI instead of a hardcoded word.
    const targetLabel = root.dataset['targetLabel'] ?? '';
    // The lesson a passed predict session credits (M8.2). Emitted by the build
    // only for a `/learn/{slug}` page that also has a predictor, so it is empty
    // on /about and in the dev gallery — where the practice still works and
    // nothing is written, because there is no lesson to credit.
    const lessonSlug = root.dataset['slug'] ?? '';

    // Query the pieces we drive.
    const canvas = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-viz-canvas]');
    const explain = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-viz-explain]');
    const metricsBox = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-viz-metrics]');
    const slider = root.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-viz-slider]');
    const counter = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-viz-counter]');
    const playBtn = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-viz-play]');
    const backBtn = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-viz-back]');
    const fwdBtn = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-viz-forward]');
    const resetBtn = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-viz-reset]');
    const speedSel = root.querySelector<HTMLSelectElement>('[data-viz-speed]');
    const form = root.querySelector<HTMLFormElement>('[data-viz-form]');
    const arrayInput = root.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-viz-array]');
    const targetInput =
      root.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-viz-target]');
    const runBtn = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-viz-run]');
    const restoreBtn =
      root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-viz-restore]');
    const errorBox = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-viz-error]');
    const errorText = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-viz-error-text]');
    // Both absent unless the BUILD found real questions in this run's trace.
    const predictBtn =
      root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-viz-predict]');
    const predictNote = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>(
      '[data-viz-predict-note]',
    );

    if (!canvas || !explain || !slider) return;

    // Loading state (M7.2). Set HERE, from JS, and never in SSR: markup-level
    // `aria-busy` would leave a JS-off page permanently "busy" for assistive
    // tech, and the JS-off reader is not waiting for anything — they have the
    // still. `aria-busy` itself waits until mount() actually starts (below):
    // an island four screens away is not loading, it is deferred, and claiming
    // otherwise would make every lesson page announce several busy regions.
    root.dataset['vizLoading'] = 'true';
    if (counter) counter.textContent = '…';

    // --- Teardown contract (TD-4): one AbortController drives every listener. ---
    const controller = new AbortController();
    const { signal } = controller;
    let player: Player<unknown> | null = null;
    let algo: Algorithm<unknown, unknown> | null = null;
    let renderer: Renderer<unknown> | null = null;
    let observer: IntersectionObserver | null = null;
    let destroyed = false;
    /** Pending rAF for the resize-driven overflow measurement; `null` when idle. */
    let measureFrame: number | null = null;
    /**
     * The `viewBox` the last measurement ran against, so a step that leaves the
     * drawing's geometry alone costs one string compare (RSP-2, below).
     */
    let measuredViewBox: string | null = null;
    /**
     * The PARSED input and the trace it produced — the two things `predictStep`
     * needs beside the current index (M8.2). They live in the island's closure
     * rather than inside `mount()` because a custom run replaces both, and the
     * Player deliberately keeps its trace private. Nothing here re-runs the
     * algorithm: this is the same trace object the Player is indexing into.
     */
    let input: unknown;
    let currentTrace: Trace<unknown> = [];
    /** The loaded algorithm's predictor, bound to it; `null` when it has none. */
    let predictFn: Predictor | null = null;
    /**
     * Whether predict mode is on. In memory and NOWHERE else: the toggle has no
     * storage key, by design (`docs/m8-gamification.md`), so it is per page
     * visit and no preference can be rewritten by a link.
     */
    let predictOn = false;
    /** The injected strip and its three parts; built on first use, never in SSR. */
    let strip: HTMLElement | null = null;
    let predictPrompt: HTMLElement | null = null;
    let predictChoices: HTMLElement | null = null;
    let predictActivity: HTMLElement | null = null;
    /**
     * This session's counts. `correct` is never displayed and never stored — it
     * exists only to compute the ≥80% bar silently (see `recordSessionPass`);
     * `answered` and `skipped` are the ACTIVITY the chip shows.
     */
    let answered = 0;
    let correct = 0;
    let skipped = 0;
    /** Whether this session has already met the bar, so the write happens once. */
    let passRecorded = false;
    /** A graded prediction waiting to ride the next explanation into the live region. */
    let pendingVerdict: 'right' | 'wrong' | null = null;
    /** The current step's explanation without any verdict prefix. */
    let lastText = explain.textContent ?? '';

    function teardown(): void {
      if (destroyed) return;
      destroyed = true;
      // Predict leaves nothing behind: the injected strip goes with the island
      // and the SSR toggle returns to the unpressed state the server rendered,
      // so a re-init after a View Transition starts where a first paint does.
      // There is nothing persisted to clear — that is the point of the toggle.
      setPredict(false);
      strip?.remove();
      strip = null;
      observer?.disconnect();
      controller.abort(); // removes every {signal}-bound listener
      if (measureFrame !== null) cancelAnimationFrame(measureFrame);
      player?.pause(); // clears any pending auto-play tick
      renderer?.destroy();
      root.removeAttribute('data-viz-ready');
      root.removeAttribute('aria-busy');
      delete root.dataset['vizLoading'];
      delete root.dataset['vizFailed'];
      delete root.dataset['vizInit']; // allow re-init after a View Transition
    }
    teardowns.set(root, teardown);

    /** Renders the metrics pills for the current step. */
    function renderMetrics(step: Step<unknown>): void {
      if (!showMetrics || !metricsBox) return;
      const metrics = step.metrics ?? {};
      metricsBox.replaceChildren();
      for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(metrics)) {
        const labelText = key[0]!.toUpperCase() + key.slice(1);
        const pill = document.createElement('span');
        pill.className = 'viz-pill';
        pill.append(`${labelText} `);
        const b = document.createElement('b');
        b.textContent = String(value);
        pill.appendChild(b);
        metricsBox.appendChild(pill);
      }
    }

    /**
     * A11Y-1: marks a transport control unavailable WITHOUT taking it out of the
     * tab order or out from under the user's focus. `disabled` on the focused
     * element sends focus to `<body>`, which costs a keyboard user their place
     * AND kills the island's Space/←/→ shortcuts (they are bound to the viz
     * root). `aria-disabled` says the same thing to assistive tech, CSS paints
     * the same unavailable state, and the click handlers no-op.
     */
    function setBound(btn: HTMLButtonElement | null, bound: boolean): void {
      btn?.setAttribute('aria-disabled', bound ? 'true' : 'false');
    }

    /** Whether a control is currently at a bound (and so must ignore activation). */
    function isBound(btn: HTMLButtonElement | null): boolean {
      return btn?.getAttribute('aria-disabled') === 'true';
    }

    /**
     * Runs a transport action only while its control is available. EVERY route
     * to the transport goes through here — the click handlers and the Space/←/→
     * shortcuts alike — because the `.viz-keys` hint advertises the keys as
     * equivalent to the buttons, and a key that acted while the button it mirrors
     * reads "unavailable" would make the bound state a lie. `aria-disabled`
     * (A11Y-1) blocks nothing on its own, so the guard has to be in the handler.
     */
    function activate(btn: HTMLButtonElement | null, run: () => void): void {
      if (!isBound(btn)) run();
    }

    /** Sets a button's accessible name and its matching hover tooltip together. */
    function setButtonLabel(btn: HTMLButtonElement, name: string): void {
      btn.setAttribute('aria-label', name);
      btn.setAttribute('title', name);
    }

    /** Mark transport buttons available/at-bound for the position (design §4). */
    function updateButtons(
      index: number,
      length: number,
      playing: boolean,
    ): void {
      const atStart = index <= 0;
      const atEnd = index >= length - 1;
      setBound(backBtn, atStart);
      setBound(resetBtn, atStart);
      setBound(fwdBtn, atEnd);
      if (playBtn) {
        // VIZ-4: at the end the button REPLAYS (Player.play seeks to 0 first),
        // so it stays available there; only a trace with nothing to advance
        // through is bound. This is also the single writer of the play button's
        // accessible name — onPlayStateChange routes back through here so the
        // two can never disagree about the state.
        //
        // M8.2: predict mode is the second reason play can be unavailable, and
        // it has to be applied HERE because this function re-decides the state
        // on every step — deciding it only in `setPredict` would leave the
        // button available again one step later.
        setBound(playBtn, length < 2 || predictOn);
        setButtonLabel(
          playBtn,
          playing ? 'Pause' : atEnd ? 'Replay from start' : 'Play',
        );
      }
    }

    // ---- Predict-the-Step (M8.2) ------------------------------------------
    // Everything below reads the SAME `currentTrace` the Player is playing and
    // asks the algorithm's own `predictStep` about it. No second trace, no
    // algorithm logic in the island, no timers: predict is a reading of the
    // pipeline, never a fork of it (spec §11.3).

    /**
     * The question for the CURRENT index, or `null` when there is nothing worth
     * predicting here (including the last step, which has no successor).
     *
     * The 2–4 choice contract the build enforces for the authored run is
     * re-checked at runtime because a custom input reaches states the build
     * never saw: a one-button "prediction" — the shape the design's floor guard
     * exists to prevent on BFS's near-path graph — is treated as no question
     * rather than rendered.
     */
    function currentQuestion(): PredictQuestion | null {
      if (!predictFn || !player) return null;
      const question = predictFn(currentTrace, player.currentIndex, input);
      if (!question || !Array.isArray(question.choices)) return null;
      const size = question.choices.length;
      return size >= 2 &&
        size <= 4 &&
        question.correctIndex >= 0 &&
        question.correctIndex < size
        ? question
        : null;
    }

    /**
     * Builds the strip once, immediately ABOVE the explanation — at runtime, and
     * only at runtime. The `<noscript>` kill-switch hides `.viz-controls`, not
     * `.viz-frame`, so a server-rendered strip would be the one gamification
     * affordance that survived with JS off, offering a question nothing on the
     * page could grade.
     */
    function buildStrip(): void {
      if (strip) return;
      // `explain` already carries a per-island unique id (the aria-live region
      // is referenced by nothing else), so deriving from it needs no second
      // uid channel and stays unique when a page hosts several visualizers.
      const promptId = `${explain!.id}-predict`;
      strip = document.createElement('div');
      strip.className = 'viz-predict';
      strip.dataset['vizPredictStrip'] = '';
      predictPrompt = document.createElement('p');
      predictPrompt.className = 'viz-predict__prompt';
      predictPrompt.id = promptId;
      predictChoices = document.createElement('div');
      predictChoices.className = 'viz-predict__choices';
      // A labelled group, so the choices are announced as one question's answers
      // and the label follows the question with no second write.
      predictChoices.setAttribute('role', 'group');
      predictChoices.setAttribute('aria-labelledby', promptId);
      predictActivity = document.createElement('p');
      predictActivity.className = 'viz-pill viz-predict__activity';
      predictActivity.hidden = true;
      strip.append(predictPrompt, predictChoices, predictActivity);
      // One delegated listener for all four buttons, torn down with the island.
      predictChoices.addEventListener(
        'click',
        (event) => {
          const btn = (event.target as HTMLElement | null)?.closest('button');
          if (btn) answer(Number(btn.dataset['choice']));
        },
        { signal },
      );
      explain!.before(strip);
    }

    /** Draws the question (or the idle line) for wherever the player now is. */
    function renderStrip(): void {
      if (!strip || !predictOn) return;
      const question = currentQuestion();
      strip.toggleAttribute('data-idle', !question);
      predictPrompt!.textContent = question ? question.prompt : PREDICT_IDLE;
      paintChoices(question);
    }

    /**
     * Reconciles the choice buttons in place.
     *
     * Reusing the existing elements is what keeps a keyboard user's focus alive
     * across the redraw that follows their own answer: replacing the buttons
     * would drop focus on `<body>`, which is exactly the failure A11Y-1 removed
     * from the transport. Only a question with FEWER choices can pull the
     * focused button out from under them, and that case is caught explicitly.
     */
    function paintChoices(question: PredictQuestion | null): void {
      const box = predictChoices!;
      const held = box.contains(document.activeElement);
      const want = question ? question.choices.length : 0;
      while (box.childElementCount > want) box.lastElementChild!.remove();
      while (box.childElementCount < want) {
        const btn = document.createElement('button');
        btn.type = 'button';
        btn.className = 'btn-secondary viz-predict__choice';
        box.appendChild(btn);
      }
      question?.choices.forEach((label, i) => {
        const btn = box.children[i] as HTMLButtonElement;
        btn.dataset['choice'] = String(i);
        btn.textContent = label;
      });
      box.hidden = want === 0;
      if (held && !box.contains(document.activeElement)) {
        // Land on the first choice of the new question, or — when this step has
        // none — on the control that carries the reader onward.
        const fallback =
          (box.firstElementChild as HTMLElement | null) ?? fwdBtn;
        fallback?.focus();
      }
    }

    /**
     * The session line: how much the reader DID, never how well.
     *
     * "7 answered · 2 skipped" is a binding calm invariant — no ratio, no
     * percentage, no score. A beginner shown an accuracy figure starts
     * protecting it instead of attempting the hard predictions, and a
     * wrong-but-attempted prediction is this mechanic working, not failing.
     * Hidden until there is something to report, so a fresh session opens with
     * no zeros staring back.
     */
    function paintActivity(): void {
      if (!predictActivity) return;
      predictActivity.hidden = answered + skipped === 0;
      predictActivity.textContent = `${answered} answered · ${skipped} skipped`;
    }

    /**
     * Grades one prediction and moves on.
     *
     * The verdict does not get its own panel: it is prefixed to the NEXT step's
     * authored explanation, so a correct and an incorrect answer both land on
     * the same immediate, elaborated feedback. Nothing here costs the reader
     * anything for being wrong — that first wrong attempt is the intervention.
     */
    function answer(choice: number): void {
      const question = currentQuestion();
      if (!question || !player) return;
      answered += 1;
      const right = choice === question.correctIndex;
      if (right) correct += 1;
      pendingVerdict = right ? 'right' : 'wrong';
      player.stepForward();
      // A predictor is contracted to return `null` on the last step, so the step
      // above always lands and `onStep` has already spoken the verdict. If a
      // custom run ever proves otherwise, say it anyway rather than swallow it.
      if (pendingVerdict) writeExplanation(lastText);
      paintActivity();
      recordSessionPass();
    }

    /**
     * Step forward, counting an on-screen question the reader chose not to
     * answer as an explicit SKIP — never as a wrong answer.
     *
     * Skipping is a first-class option (autonomy is the design's stated reason),
     * so BOTH routes to the control go through here: the button and the → key.
     */
    function stepForwardCounted(): void {
      if (predictOn && currentQuestion()) {
        skipped += 1;
        paintActivity();
      }
      player?.stepForward();
    }

    /**
     * The predict path onto the mastery ladder: ≥5 answers at ≥80% in this
     * session (`docs/m8-gamification.md` M8.2).
     *
     * Computed in integers — the ratio is never formatted, let alone displayed —
     * and written through `recordPass`, which owns the 3-day Mastered gate, so
     * predict re-implements no promotion rule. Nothing about the attempts is
     * stored: the session is infinitely retriable and its history is not the
     * product's business.
     *
     * The store is imported on demand so that its ~2 KB gz is not in the bundle
     * of every page that merely SHOWS a visualization (/about ships one and has
     * no lesson to credit). On a lesson page `PracticeCheck` imports the same
     * module statically, so the chunk is already loaded and this resolves out of
     * the module registry rather than the network — which is also why a failed
     * import here is treated as "not saved yet" rather than an error state.
     */
    function recordSessionPass(): void {
      if (
        passRecorded ||
        !lessonSlug ||
        answered < 5 ||
        correct * 5 < answered * 4
      ) {
        return;
      }
      passRecorded = true;
      void import('../lib/progress')
        .then((store) => {
          store.recordPass(lessonSlug);
        })
        .catch(() => {
          // Nothing on screen ever claimed this was saved, and the bar can be
          // met again in the same session — so the only repair needed is to let
          // the next answer try the write again.
          passRecorded = false;
        });
    }

    /**
     * Turns predict mode on or off. The single writer of every piece of state it
     * touches, so the toggle, the review deep link and teardown cannot disagree.
     */
    function setPredict(on: boolean): void {
      if (!predictBtn || predictOn === on) return;
      predictOn = on;
      predictBtn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', on ? 'true' : 'false');
      if (on) {
        // Predicting and watching are incompatible, so stop any run first.
        player?.pause();
        // A fresh session each time it is switched on. Nothing is carried over
        // and nothing was written down, which is what "infinitely retriable"
        // means in practice.
        answered = 0;
        correct = 0;
        skipped = 0;
        passRecorded = false;
        buildStrip();
      }
      // A11Y-1 again: auto-play and the scrubber become unavailable through
      // `aria-disabled` plus the note that says why — never the `disabled`
      // attribute, which would drop a focused control's focus on `<body>` and
      // take the island's own shortcuts with it.
      slider!.setAttribute('aria-disabled', on ? 'true' : 'false');
      if (predictNote) predictNote.hidden = !on;
      for (const el of [playBtn, slider]) {
        if (!el || !predictNote) continue;
        if (on) el.setAttribute('aria-describedby', predictNote.id);
        else el.removeAttribute('aria-describedby');
      }
      if (strip) strip.hidden = !on;
      paintActivity();
      renderStrip();
      updateButtons(player?.currentIndex ?? 0, player?.length ?? 1, false);
    }

    /**
     * A11Y-6 (spec §10's M7 exception): above 1× the trace advances every ~300ms
     * or faster, so a polite live region either floods the reader or drops most
     * of it — either way it is unusable and it drowns out the rest of the page.
     * Mute it for exactly that case. Every manual step, seek and pause restores
     * it (they all pause first, and this runs on the pause), and `onStep`
     * unmutes BEFORE writing the last step so the one announcement that matters —
     * where the run ended — still lands, once.
     */
    function syncLiveMute(): void {
      const mute = (player?.isPlaying ?? false) && (player?.speed ?? 1) > 1;
      explain!.setAttribute('aria-live', mute ? 'off' : 'polite');
    }

    /**
     * Applies a speed multiplier to THIS island's player. The single place that
     * calls `setSpeed`, because that method fires no callback and the live-region
     * policy therefore has to be re-evaluated by hand: a speed change mid-run
     * crosses the 1× mute threshold in both directions (A11Y-6). Used by the
     * `<select>`, by the sibling-sync listener, and by the stored-preference
     * restore, so none of the three can forget it.
     */
    function applySpeed(multiplier: number): void {
      player?.setSpeed(multiplier);
      syncLiveMute();
    }

    /**
     * Writes one step's explanation into the live region — and, when a
     * prediction has just been graded, the verdict with it, as ONE change.
     *
     * One write, not two: the region is `aria-atomic`, so a separate verdict
     * write would either be announced as a second, competing utterance or be
     * overwritten before it was spoken. The verdict is carried by a glyph AND a
     * word, never by colour (§12), and the glyph is `aria-hidden` so the
     * announcement reads "Correct — <the step's own explanation>".
     */
    function writeExplanation(text: string): void {
      lastText = text;
      if (!pendingVerdict) {
        explain!.textContent = text;
        return;
      }
      const right = pendingVerdict === 'right';
      pendingVerdict = null;
      const tag = document.createElement('span');
      tag.className = 'viz-verdict';
      tag.dataset['verdict'] = right ? 'right' : 'wrong';
      const glyph = document.createElement('span');
      glyph.className = 'viz-verdict__glyph';
      glyph.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
      glyph.textContent = right ? '✓' : '✗';
      // "Not quite" and not "Wrong": the wording never makes a first miss cost
      // something, because eliciting that miss is the point of predicting.
      tag.append(glyph, right ? 'Correct' : 'Not quite');
      explain!.replaceChildren(tag, ` — ${text}`);
    }

    /** onStep: sync explanation, slider, counter, metrics, and button states. */
    function onStep(index: number, step: Step<unknown>): void {
      const length = player?.length ?? 1;
      // Unmute before the FINAL write, not after it: flipping aria-live back
      // afterwards announces nothing, because the region's content stopped
      // changing at the same moment (A11Y-6).
      if (index >= length - 1) explain!.setAttribute('aria-live', 'polite');
      writeExplanation(step.explanation);
      slider!.value = String(index);
      slider!.setAttribute('aria-valuetext', `Step ${index + 1} of ${length}`);
      // VD-4: one custom-property write per step drives the track's elapsed
      // fill; the gradient that reads it lives in CSS, so nothing paints here.
      const progress = length > 1 ? (index / (length - 1)) * 100 : 0;
      slider!.style.setProperty('--viz-progress', `${progress.toFixed(2)}%`);
      if (counter) counter.textContent = `${index + 1} / ${length}`;
      renderMetrics(step);
      updateButtons(index, length, player?.isPlaying ?? false);
      // The question belongs to the index, so it is re-asked wherever the reader
      // lands — by answering, by stepping, or by running a new input.
      renderStrip();
      // The renderer has just redrawn: if that widened the drawing, the RSP-2
      // floor and the scroll-region state both have to be recomputed for it.
      remeasureIfResized();
    }

    /** onPlayStateChange: swap the Play/Pause glyph, then refresh button state. */
    function onPlayStateChange(playing: boolean): void {
      // Live-region policy first: this fires on pause BEFORE the step that
      // caused it is drawn (every Player method pauses, then draws), so the
      // restored region is in place for that step's announcement.
      syncLiveMute();
      if (!playBtn) return;
      // data-playing drives the CSS glyph swap. No aria-pressed (VIZ-9/A11Y-7):
      // the accessible name carries the state, and updateButtons owns that name.
      playBtn.toggleAttribute('data-playing', playing);
      updateButtons(player?.currentIndex ?? 0, player?.length ?? 1, playing);
    }

    /** Shows a friendly custom-input error without disturbing the current trace. */
    function showError(message: string): void {
      if (errorBox && errorText) {
        // Reveal BEFORE writing. `hidden` is display:none, which keeps the <p>
        // out of the accessibility tree entirely, so text written while it is
        // hidden is not a change to a live region and most screen readers stay
        // silent; unhiding first gives them a region to observe.
        errorBox.hidden = false;
        errorText.textContent = message;
      }
      // Blame the field the message is about; when the target field isn't
      // rendered, every error is the first field's by construction.
      const field =
        errorField(message, targetLabel) === 'target' && targetInput
          ? targetInput
          : arrayInput;
      // Exactly one field is invalid at a time — a stale flag left on the other
      // would mis-describe a field the reader is about to correct.
      arrayInput?.removeAttribute('aria-invalid');
      targetInput?.removeAttribute('aria-invalid');
      field?.setAttribute('aria-invalid', 'true');
      // Moving focus re-announces the field together with this message via
      // aria-describedby, so the reader still gets it if the polite live-region
      // update is dropped (A11Y-8).
      field?.focus();
    }

    /** Clears a previously shown error from BOTH fields. */
    function clearError(): void {
      if (errorBox) errorBox.hidden = true;
      // Blank the text as well: a hidden node that aria-describedby names
      // DIRECTLY still contributes to the accessible description (accname §4.3.2
      // step 2A), so a resolved error would keep being announced with both
      // fields for the rest of the session.
      if (errorText) errorText.textContent = '';
      arrayInput?.removeAttribute('aria-invalid');
      targetInput?.removeAttribute('aria-invalid');
    }

    /** Points the slider bounds at the loaded trace and refreshes controls. */
    function syncTraceBounds(): void {
      if (!player) return;
      slider!.max = String(Math.max(player.length - 1, 0));
      slider!.disabled = false;
      measureCanvas();
    }

    /**
     * Announces a finished custom run to the page (`viz:run`; spec §11.3's M8
     * amendment, contract in `src/lib/challenges.ts`).
     *
     * CONSUMES, NEVER RECOMPUTES: the payload's `finalStep` is the last element
     * of the trace this island just computed and the Player is already indexing
     * into — the same object, not a copy and not a second run. No mechanic's
     * logic enters the pipeline here; a Trace Trial grades what the reader
     * watched, which is the whole reason the event carries a step at all.
     *
     * Fired ONLY from the custom-input submit path. Deliberately NOT on mount —
     * a trial has to be earned by crafting an input, not by loading the page —
     * and deliberately NOT from "Restore example", which replays the AUTHORED
     * run: the challenge build guard already proves the lesson's own example
     * clears nothing, so announcing it could only ever write "Not yet" onto a
     * card in answer to a recovery click.
     *
     * Adds no listener and no timer, so the island's teardown contract (TD-4)
     * is untouched: a dispatch owns nothing that could outlive the island.
     *
     * @param raw - The exact string this run's `parseInput` accepted, so a
     * consumer reads back the input the reader really submitted.
     */
    function announceRun(raw: string): void {
      // An empty trace has nothing to grade. Unreachable for a parsed input in
      // practice, but a listener must never be handed an `undefined` step it
      // would then have to defend against.
      const finalStep = currentTrace[currentTrace.length - 1];
      if (!finalStep) return;
      // Bubbling per the contract even though it is dispatched on `document`:
      // the cards listen there today, and a future consumer on `window` must
      // still hear it.
      document.dispatchEvent(
        new CustomEvent<VizRunDetail>(VIZ_RUN_EVENT, {
          bubbles: true,
          detail: { algorithmId: algoId, input: raw, finalStep },
        }),
      );
    }

    /**
     * RSP-2: gives the drawing a legibility floor and keeps the resulting scroll
     * container operable. The floor itself is CSS (`min-width` from
     * `--viz-natural-w`); this reads the LIVE `viewBox` — the renderer rewrites
     * it whenever the item count changes — and then reconciles the canvas's two
     * competing tabindex needs against reality:
     *
     * - not overflowing → `tabindex="-1"`: a click target for the Space/←/→
     *   shortcuts, invisible to assistive tech and absent from the tab order;
     * - overflowing → a real scroll region, so `tabindex="0"` + `role="group"` +
     *   a name, because a scrollable box no one can reach with a keyboard is a
     *   2.1.1 failure — a worse trade than the reflow it was fixing.
     */
    function measureCanvas(): void {
      const svg = canvas!.querySelector('svg');
      if (svg instanceof SVGSVGElement) {
        // Remember the geometry this pass ran against (remeasureIfResized).
        measuredViewBox = svg.getAttribute('viewBox');
        const natural = svg.viewBox.baseVal.width;
        if (natural > 0) {
          canvas!.style.setProperty(
            '--viz-natural-w',
            `${Math.round(natural)}px`,
          );
        }
      }
      // 1px of slack: sub-pixel layout rounding otherwise reports a permanent
      // overflow on boxes that visually fit exactly.
      const overflows = canvas!.scrollWidth - canvas!.clientWidth > 1;
      canvas!.setAttribute('tabindex', overflows ? '0' : '-1');
      if (overflows) {
        canvas!.setAttribute('role', 'group');
        canvas!.setAttribute('aria-label', `${title}, scrollable diagram`);
      } else {
        canvas!.removeAttribute('role');
        canvas!.removeAttribute('aria-label');
      }
    }

    /**
     * RSP-2 upkeep. Several renderers GROW mid-trace — a tree gains a level, a
     * hash bucket gains a chain link, an array insert adds a cell — and both the
     * legibility floor and the scroll-region reconciliation are computed from the
     * `viewBox`, so a single mount-time measurement goes stale on exactly the
     * lessons that need it most. Reading the attribute is a string compare that
     * touches no layout, so it can run on every step; the full measurement (which
     * reads `scrollWidth` and therefore forces a synchronous reflow) runs only
     * when the drawing really changed shape.
     */
    function remeasureIfResized(): void {
      const svg = canvas!.querySelector('svg');
      if (!(svg instanceof SVGSVGElement)) return;
      if (svg.getAttribute('viewBox') === measuredViewBox) return;
      measureCanvas();
    }

    /** Renders the terminal "failed" state (chunk load / bad input — TD defense). */
    function renderUnavailable(): void {
      // Distinguishable from "loading" (VIZ-*): the controls are hidden rather
      // than dimmed, the busy flag is cleared, and the copy says what the reader
      // can still do instead of only naming the failure.
      delete root.dataset['vizLoading'];
      root.removeAttribute('aria-busy');
      root.dataset['vizFailed'] = 'true';
      canvas!.replaceChildren();
      const box = document.createElement('p');
      box.className = 'viz-unavailable';
      box.textContent =
        'This visualization could not load. Reload the page to try again — the steps are also described in the text below.';
      canvas!.appendChild(box);
    }

    /** The mount sequence (architecture §5.3). */
    async function mount(): Promise<void> {
      // Now it really is loading: the chunks are about to be fetched.
      root.setAttribute('aria-busy', 'true');
      const algoThunk = (
        algorithms as Record<string, () => Promise<Algorithm<unknown, unknown>>>
      )[algoId];
      const rendererThunk = (
        renderers as Record<string, () => Promise<RendererModule<unknown>>>
      )[rendererId];
      if (!algoThunk || !rendererThunk) {
        renderUnavailable();
        return;
      }

      let rmod: RendererModule<unknown>;
      try {
        algo = await algoThunk();
        rmod = await rendererThunk();
      } catch {
        renderUnavailable();
        return;
      }
      if (destroyed) return; // torn down while awaiting the chunks

      // Resolve initial input: authored `input` prop, else defaultInput(). Both
      // it and the trace it produces are kept in the island's closure, because
      // `predictStep(trace, i, input)` needs them on every step (M8.2).
      if (rawInput) {
        const parsed = algo.parseInput(rawInput);
        if (isParseError(parsed)) {
          renderUnavailable();
          return;
        }
        input = parsed;
      } else {
        input = algo.defaultInput();
      }

      // The predictor, bound to its algorithm so one written as a method keeps
      // its `this`. The build already proved this one exists and asks real
      // questions about this run; if a loaded chunk ever disagrees, the toggle
      // cannot work, so it goes rather than sitting there asking nothing.
      predictFn = algo.predictStep?.bind(algo) ?? null;
      if (!predictFn) predictBtn?.remove();

      const trace = algo.run(input);
      currentTrace = trace;
      renderer = rmod.create();
      // Replace the static still, then mount the live renderer in its place.
      canvas!.replaceChildren();
      renderer.mount(canvas!, { title });

      player = new Player<unknown>({
        trace,
        renderer,
        onStep,
        onPlayStateChange,
      });
      player.reset();
      syncTraceBounds();
      wireControls();
      delete root.dataset['vizLoading'];
      root.removeAttribute('aria-busy');
      root.setAttribute('data-viz-ready', 'true');
    }

    /** Wires every control to the player once it exists (all listeners aborted on teardown). */
    function wireControls(): void {
      // Bound-state guards (A11Y-1): the button stays focusable and focused at a
      // bound, so its handler is what has to decline. Player clamps every method
      // anyway — this only avoids the pointless redraw and keeps the UI honest.
      playBtn?.addEventListener(
        'click',
        () => activate(playBtn, () => player?.toggle()),
        { signal },
      );
      backBtn?.addEventListener(
        'click',
        () => activate(backBtn, () => player?.stepBackward()),
        { signal },
      );
      fwdBtn?.addEventListener(
        'click',
        () => activate(fwdBtn, stepForwardCounted),
        { signal },
      );
      resetBtn?.addEventListener(
        'click',
        () => activate(resetBtn, () => player?.reset()),
        { signal },
      );
      slider!.addEventListener(
        'input',
        () => {
          // `aria-disabled` blocks nothing by itself (A11Y-1), so the handler
          // declines while predicting and puts the thumb back where the trace
          // actually is — scrubbing past a question is the one thing predict
          // mode exists to prevent.
          if (predictOn) {
            slider!.value = String(player?.currentIndex ?? 0);
            return;
          }
          player?.seek(Number(slider!.value));
        },
        { signal },
      );
      predictBtn?.addEventListener('click', () => setPredict(!predictOn), {
        signal,
      });
      speedSel?.addEventListener(
        'change',
        () => {
          applySpeed(Number(speedSel.value));
          storeSpeed(speedSel.value);
          // One preference, N islands: tell the siblings, so the visualizer
          // further down the lesson doesn't keep running at the old rate
          // (SPEED_EVENT). Only a real `change` reaches here, so this never
          // echoes the listener below.
          document.dispatchEvent(
            new CustomEvent(SPEED_EVENT, { detail: speedSel.value }),
          );
        },
        { signal },
      );

      // Sibling sync. Bound with {signal} like every other listener, so teardown
      // (TD-4) unsubscribes this island from the document automatically — a
      // document-level listener is exactly the kind that outlives a View
      // Transition otherwise.
      document.addEventListener(
        SPEED_EVENT,
        (event) => {
          // Normalized, not trusted: the detail decides what a `<select>` shows,
          // and only an option this build offers can be displayed.
          const speed = normalizeSpeed((event as CustomEvent<string>).detail);
          if (speed === null || !speedSel) return;
          const value = String(speed);
          // Re-entrancy guard: on the island that dispatched, the control
          // already holds this value, so there is nothing left to do.
          if (speedSel.value === value) return;
          speedSel.value = value;
          applySpeed(speed);
        },
        { signal },
      );

      // Enable controls now that a player exists. The custom-input fields + Run
      // button ship `disabled` in SSR (A3) so a pre-hydration click can't submit
      // the actionless form and trigger a full-page reload; enable them here.
      // From this point on, transport availability is aria-disabled only (A11Y-1).
      [
        playBtn,
        backBtn,
        fwdBtn,
        resetBtn,
        predictBtn,
        speedSel,
        arrayInput,
        targetInput,
        runBtn,
        restoreBtn,
      ].forEach((el) => {
        if (el) el.disabled = false;
      });
      updateButtons(player?.currentIndex ?? 0, player?.length ?? 1, false);

      // The review queue's deep link (M8.2): `?review=1` opens predict mode for
      // THIS page visit and nothing else. It cannot rewrite a preference because
      // there is none to rewrite — the toggle is never persisted, which is
      // precisely why the design routes the review card through a query
      // parameter instead of a stored flag.
      if (
        predictBtn &&
        new URLSearchParams(location.search).get('review') === '1'
      ) {
        setPredict(true);
      }

      // Restore the reader's speed preference (§6 `pref:viz-speed`). Applied
      // AFTER the enable pass so the <select> is already interactive, and only
      // when it names an option this build still offers.
      const storedSpeed = readStoredSpeed();
      if (storedSpeed !== null && speedSel) {
        speedSel.value = String(storedSpeed);
        applySpeed(storedSpeed);
      }

      // Custom-input flow (§5.4): recompute on valid, keep trace on invalid.
      form?.addEventListener(
        'submit',
        (event) => {
          event.preventDefault();
          if (!algo || !player) return;
          const raw = `${arrayInput?.value ?? ''} target=${targetInput?.value ?? ''}`;
          const parsed = algo.parseInput(raw);
          if (isParseError(parsed)) {
            showError(parsed.error);
            return;
          }
          clearError();
          // The parsed input and its trace are what predict grades against, so
          // both move with the run (M8.2). The trace is still computed once and
          // only indexed into — this is the same object the Player receives.
          input = parsed;
          currentTrace = algo.run(parsed);
          player.loadTrace(currentTrace);
          syncTraceBounds();
          // The reader crafted a run and it succeeded: tell the page (M8.3).
          // Last, so anything listening sees a settled island — and only here,
          // because this is the only path where the input is the reader's own.
          announceRun(raw);
        },
        { signal },
      );

      // Recovery (CMP-14): re-run the AUTHORED example and put its real values
      // back in the fields, so a broken experiment is one click from the lesson's
      // own picture. The authored string parsed at build time, so the failure
      // branch here is unreachable in practice — it is kept because a stale
      // data-input after a partial swap must not throw.
      restoreBtn?.addEventListener(
        'click',
        () => {
          if (!algo || !player) return;
          const parsed = algo.parseInput(rawInput);
          if (isParseError(parsed)) return;
          const fields = splitAuthoredInput(rawInput);
          if (arrayInput) arrayInput.value = fields.input;
          if (targetInput) targetInput.value = fields.target;
          clearError();
          input = parsed;
          currentTrace = algo.run(parsed);
          player.loadTrace(currentTrace);
          syncTraceBounds();
        },
        { signal },
      );

      // Click-then-Space (design §3.3): the shortcuts are bound to the viz root
      // and only fire while focus is inside it, so clicking the drawing has to
      // put focus there. preventScroll because the click already established
      // where the reader is looking.
      canvas!.addEventListener(
        'pointerdown',
        () => canvas!.focus({ preventScroll: true }),
        { signal },
      );

      // RSP-2: the overflow verdict depends on the box width, so re-measure when
      // the viewport changes. One rAF-coalesced pass per burst — resize fires far
      // faster than layout is worth reading.
      window.addEventListener(
        'resize',
        () => {
          if (measureFrame !== null) return;
          measureFrame = requestAnimationFrame(() => {
            measureFrame = null;
            if (!destroyed) measureCanvas();
          });
        },
        { signal },
      );

      // Convenience keys inside the viz (design §3.3): Space=toggle, ←/→=step.
      root.addEventListener(
        'keydown',
        (event) => {
          const target = event.target as HTMLElement;
          const tag = target.tagName;
          if (tag === 'INPUT' || tag === 'SELECT' || tag === 'TEXTAREA') return;
          if (event.key === ' ' || event.key === 'Spacebar') {
            if (tag === 'BUTTON') return; // let the button's native click fire
            event.preventDefault();
            activate(playBtn, () => player?.toggle());
          } else if (event.key === 'ArrowLeft' || event.key === 'ArrowRight') {
            // While the canvas is a real scroll region (RSP-2), ←/→ belong to
            // the scroll container: stealing them would leave its content
            // unreachable by keyboard (2.1.1). Everywhere else they step —
            // through the SAME availability gate as the buttons they mirror, so
            // a key can never do what the matching control declines to do. The
            // key is still swallowed at a bound: the page must not scroll
            // sideways as a consolation for a step that didn't happen.
            if (canvas!.tabIndex === 0 && canvas!.contains(target)) return;
            event.preventDefault();
            if (event.key === 'ArrowLeft')
              activate(backBtn, () => player?.stepBackward());
            // The SAME skip-counting route as the button it mirrors (M8.2), so
            // the key can never advance past a question without counting it.
            else activate(fwdBtn, stepForwardCounted);
          }
        },
        { signal },
      );
    }

    // Defer the heavy work until the viz scrolls near the viewport.
    if ('IntersectionObserver' in window) {
      observer = new IntersectionObserver(
        (entries, obs) => {
          for (const entry of entries) {
            if (entry.isIntersecting) {
              obs.disconnect();
              void mount();
            }
          }
        },
        { rootMargin: '200px' },
      );
      observer.observe(root);
    } else {
      void mount();
    }
  }

  /** (Re)initialise every island on the page. */
  function initAll(): void {
    document
      .querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-viz]')
      .forEach((root) => setupViz(root));
  }

  /** Dispose every island before a View Transition swap (TD-4). */
  function teardownAll(): void {
    document
      .querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-viz]')
      .forEach((root) => teardowns.get(root)?.());
  }

  initAll();
  // View Transitions (M4): re-init the fresh DOM, dispose the outgoing one.
  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', initAll);
  document.addEventListener('astro:before-swap', teardownAll);
</script>
